@CircuitSage I pulled the trigger, couldnt help myself. 

I was under the impression that PCIe5 x8 per card should be quite enough for these cards.

If there is some stuff you would like to me try or benchmark let me know. I should have it running on Wednesday.

I bought 4 of these to play with at home and have had a surprisingly good time with intel-autoround and doing W4A16 quants to squeeze more context out of dense models.

couple jank home solutions below.

high cpu idle
VLLM_SLEEP_WHEN_IDLE=1 has a few downsides however stops a cpu thread per gpu being pinned at 100% which is nice for power and noise at home at the cost of latency when receiving a request after being idle.

high gpu fan idle
the fans still don’t ramp down when idle for me even with updated firmware and intel’s new BKC installer revision (6.14/1011-intel)

I ended up having to jank this together:

VLLM_CONFIGURE_LOGGING=1
VLLM_LOGGING_CONFIG_PATH=/home/user/scripts/logger.json

logger.json{
“formatters”: {
“json”: {
“class”: “pythonjsonlogger.jsonlogger.JsonFormatter”
}
},
“handlers”: {
“file”: {
“class”: “logging.FileHandler”,
“formatter”: “json”,
“level”: “INFO”,
“filename”: “/tmp/vllm.log”
}
},
“loggers”: {
“vllm”: {
“handlers”: \[“file”\],
“level”: “INFO”,
“propagate”: false
}
},
“version”: 1
}

#!/bin/bash

LOG_FILE=“/tmp/vllm.log”
SEARCH_STRING=“Avg prompt throughput”
COMMAND_TO_RUN=“sleep 15 && xpu-smi discovery > /dev/null”

tail -F “$LOG_FILE” | while IFS= read -r line; do
if \[\[ “$line” == *“$SEARCH_STRING”* \]\]; then
eval “$COMMAND_TO_RUN”
fi
done

you could probably get away with something as simple as triggering xpu-smi on a timer, the user needs video and render groups for this to work.

Hmmm, can you try running with INFR_CPU=1?

This should allow us to isolate if it’s crashing before or during GPU initializing

INFR_CPU=1 ./target/release/infr run {model}
